# Executable files and compiled binaries
# These are the files generated when you build your project.
# The executable name will typically be the name of your module or main package.
*.exe
*.bin
*.out
/bin/

# IDE and Editor-specific files
# These files store project-specific settings and state for your IDE.
# They are not part of the source code and shouldn't be shared.
.idea/                # IntelliJ IDEA / GoLand project files
.vscode/              # Visual Studio Code settings
.DS_Store             # macOS-specific file
*.swp                 # Vim swap files
*~                    # Emacs backup files

# Dependencies and build cache
# Go manages dependencies in the `pkg/mod` and `vendor` directories.
# The Go build cache is located in a directory specific to the OS.
# The vendor directory is usually handled by `go mod vendor` and may or may not be checked in.
# It's a common practice to not commit vendor files unless you have a specific reason to.
vendor/               # Go modules vendor directory
go.sum                # go.sum is usually committed, but if you have a specific reason not to, you can ignore it.
go.mod                # go.mod should be committed.
pkg/                  # Go build cache and module cache

# Environment variables and sensitive data
# These files often contain API keys, database passwords, or other secrets.
# They should never be committed to a public repository.
.env                  # Common for environment variables
.env.local            # Local environment variables
config.json           # If this file contains sensitive data

# Build-related directories and temporary files
# Directories that are used for building or testing
/build/
/dist/
/release/
/tmp/

# Log files and database files
# These are generated during runtime and are not part of the source.
*.log
*.sqlite
*.db

# OS-specific files
Thumbs.db             # Windows-specific thumbnail cache